Stop the reader before closing its pipe

kill() tears the ConPTY down in an order that guarantees one failed read on a
closed handle:

    closePseudoConsole(m_ptyHandler);
    CloseHandle(m_hPipeOut);
    CloseHandle(m_hPipeIn);                 // the reader is blocked in here
    m_readThread->requestInterruption();    // ... and only now is it told

Closing the pipe is what unblocks the reader -- requestInterruption() on its own
cannot, because the thread is parked inside ReadFile and never reaches the check
until the read returns. So the close has to happen. What does not have to happen
is doing it while the thread still believes it should keep going: ReadFile fails,
isInterruptionRequested() is false, and the loop goes round again on a handle
that has just been closed. 0009 makes that second pass exit rather than spin,
but the read still happens.

Setting the flag first closes the window. The failing read then finds the
interruption already requested and leaves on the first pass, so nothing re-enters
ReadFile on a closed handle -- which matters because a closed HANDLE value can be
handed straight back out to another thread by CreateFile, and a stale read would
land on whatever inherited the number.

This is ordering only; every call kill() makes is unchanged, as is the
wait(1000)/terminate() that follows.

Upstream: not submitted; belongs on PR #578, which is not being merged --
         see 0001's header
Applies-to: all environments (the changed code is behind Q_OS_WIN)

--- a/lib/ptyqt/conptyprocess.cpp
+++ b/lib/ptyqt/conptyprocess.cpp
@@ -255,6 +255,13 @@
     if (m_ptyHandler != INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE) {
         m_aboutToDestruct = true;
 
+        // Ask the reader to stop *before* taking away the handle it is blocked
+        // on. Closing the pipe is what unblocks ReadFile, so the close has to
+        // happen too -- but with the flag already set, the failing read finds
+        // isInterruptionRequested() true and leaves on the first pass instead
+        // of going round again on a closed handle.
+        m_readThread->requestInterruption();
+
         // Close ConPTY - this will terminate client process if running
         m_winContext.closePseudoConsole(m_ptyHandler);
 
@@ -263,8 +270,6 @@
             CloseHandle(m_hPipeOut);
         if (INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE != m_hPipeIn)
             CloseHandle(m_hPipeIn);
-
-        m_readThread->requestInterruption();
         if (!m_readThread->wait(1000))
             m_readThread->terminate();
         m_readThread->deleteLater();
